Start();
windows = 2;
show(tabForm);
search FORMs;
register(WindowSearchT);
define
    wsX = 0;
    wsY = 0;
    wsRead form:=tabForm;
    wsAt code={
        px = wsX;
        py = wsY;
        argument :% copyOf(px);
        argument :% copyOf(py);
        pixelAt(argument);
        };
    wsClick form:=tabForm px=180 py=62 later;
    wsBig x=400 y=400 width=500 height=500;
    wsResize form:=tabForm frame:=wsBig later;
    wsClose form:=tabForm later;
    ;
search WindowSearchT;
cout "WS-1 before the draw":;
readWindow(wsRead);
wsX = 180; wsY = 200; wsAt(wsRead);
wsX = 20; wsY = 62; wsAt(wsRead);
windowClick(wsClick);
windowResize(wsResize);
windowClose(wsClose);
cout "WS END":;
stop();

windowSearchT -- fix stroke W5-1 (2026-11-23, SEQ 754). The first window anyone saw was blank: REFUSED drawFill.
show puts the search list back when it returns, so a window's later calls into kant (a draw, a click, a resize, a
close) ran under the script's own list, with no Utilities on it; drawWalk, first built then, bound drawFill to a
fresh local. Every other window fixture includes utilities itself and keeps it on the list, so none could see it.
This one is tester's shape: the bootstrap show and nothing else, then a list holding only FORMs and this registry.
The cure: a window keeps the search list in force when it was opened and runs every call into kant under it
(guiHost.mm, runUnder).
WS-1  the window show made, read with Utilities off the list, paints: the first card's green, tab One's selected green.
WS-2  inside the real loop, after the script has ended: a click on tab Two fires tabClick, the resize lays out at
      500x500, the close keeps the frame; no REFUSED line anywhere.
